✅ Created [#85](https://github.com/roo-oliv/monodreams/issues/85) and linked it under #81.

---

## Sub-issue 5 of 13 — A3: windows bigger than the screen render offscreen (macOS) → smart window-fit in the engine

**The picture:** you ship your game at 1920×1080 fixed-size — a completely ordinary choice. A player opens it on a MacBook whose screen is 1512×982 points. macOS clamps *resizable* windows to fit, but MonoGame's default is a **fixed** window, and those macOS happily lets hang off the edge of the screen — so your title screen opens with the bottom third (where the "Start" button lives) physically below the desk. The player sees a broken game; nothing crashed, nothing logged. And you can't easily ask "how big is the usable screen?" because MonoGame never bound SDL's menu-bar-aware `SDL_GetDisplayUsableBounds` — only the raw display size.

**What NFs, Please! built (proven, ready to lift):** `WindowFit.cs` + `DesktopWindow.cs`:
- computes the **largest aspect-correct window inside the usable bounds** — calling `SDL_GetDisplayUsableBounds` directly on the SDL library MonoGame already loaded (fixed-margin fallback if the export is missing), snapped to multiples of 16, capped at the render resolution (1:1 = sharpest);
- resizable in normal play, fixed only in override/pin modes;
- an env override (`WxH`) and a screenshot-pin mode (that part stays with sub-issue 10);
- one boot log line showing display/usable/window/mode — the feature's observable;
- hard-won empirical note that must travel with the code: on macOS DesktopGL *everything* (display mode, SDL window, GL backbuffer) is in **points**, no Retina conversion anywhere.

**My proposal:** lift it into `foundation` as an opt-in windowing helper (e.g. the game hands it a target render resolution, it returns/applies the fitted window), rename the env override to `MONODREAMS_WINDOW=WxH`, and have the `init` template use it by default so every scaffolded game is immune from day one. Docs premise capturing the points-not-pixels invariant.

**Value:** every MonoDreams game stops being silently broken on laptops smaller than its render resolution — the kind of bug users *never* report, they just close the game.

Approve / reject / comment?

